   "speech": "Duplay rose and swept his bow sharply, and the hypnotist’s heart, under the force of a sudden rush of blood, began to pound painfully. At the very first sounds that flew from the strings of Duplay’s violin, Roumier understood that he must not listen any further. Those sounds blinded and cast one down. No one could have put them into words. Roumier only felt that his whole life, such as it had passed up to that day, was utterly needless to him, hateful and useless, and that under the spell of such music a man, whoever he might be, would accomplish anything with the same fury of rapture: the greatest crime and the greatest sacrifice. An anguished dread took hold of him; with an effort almost superhuman in that state, Roumier tore the violin from Duplay’s hands, feeling as though he had spat in the face of a god, and, having thus broken the annihilating enchantment, cried:",
